{"tc":{"ruleId":"afw4f7","ruleName":"Text has minimum contrast","testcaseId":"dbd2374952b96375369afe2a012bfbadd182bf6b","expected":"inapplicable","sc":["1.4.3"],"localPath":"pages/afw4f7/dbd2374952b96375369afe2a012bfbadd182bf6b.html","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-assets/wcag-act-rules/testcases/afw4f7/dbd2374952b96375369afe2a012bfbadd182bf6b.html","draft":false},"agentSubjects":[],"rubricSubjects":[{"xpath":"/html/body/p[1]","sc":"1.4.3","claimFamily":"text-contrast","rubricId":"contrast-over-complex-backdrop-v0","rubric":{"id":"contrast-over-complex-backdrop-v0","sc":"1.4.3","skill":"color-and-visual-text","visionEvidence":["element-crop","surrounding-region"],"text":"# 1.4.3 — text contrast over a complex backdrop (v0 atomic rubric)\n\n**Division of labor (v3.2).** You do NOT investigate the page, and you do NOT compute contrast ratios.\nThe deterministic C3 runner already CLEARS (or fails) every case it can compute soundly — text over a\n**flat, opaque** background, where the foreground/background colors are known and the WCAG ratio is exact.\nYou are handed only the obligations it left at auto-PARTIAL: text rendered over a **non-uniform** backdrop\nthe runner cannot reduce to two colors — a photo, a gradient, a video frame, a patterned/semi-transparent\noverlay, or text sitting partly on and partly off an image. That perceptual case is exactly what pixels\nanswer and a color computation cannot. Where a deterministic CLAIM already disposed this obligation, DEFER.\n\n**Judge:** is the text **readable** against its actual, possibly-varying background — can a sighted user\nwith low-to-normal vision comfortably make out every character — across the WHOLE run of the text, not\njust its easiest region? A clear, consistent separation between the glyphs and the backdrop (a solid\ntext-shadow/scrim, a sufficiently dark/light plate behind the text, or simply high luminance distance\neverywhere the text falls) is NOT a barrier. Text that disappears into, blends with, or is only partially\nlegible against the busy/low-contrast region of its backdrop — e.g. light text over the bright part of a\nphoto, or thin text over a same-luminance gradient — IS a barrier.\n\n**Evidence handed to you:** the `element-crop` (the text and the backdrop directly behind it) and the\n`surrounding-region` (so you can see whether a scrim/plate extends under all of the text), plus the\nelement's role/label as context. Judge from these pixels only.\n\n**WCAG soundness caveats (do NOT manufacture a failure these don't support):**\n- Do NOT estimate a numeric ratio or assert a 4.5:1 / 3:1 threshold from the image — vision cannot read\n  exact ratios, and the flat-backdrop cases that CAN be computed never reach you (the runner owns them).\n  Judge *perceivable readability*, not a measured number.\n- The WORST region governs: if the backdrop varies, a passage that is crisp over the dark third but\n  vanishes over the bright third IS a barrier — do not clear it on the strength of the readable part.\n- A solid text-shadow, scrim, semi-opaque plate, or outline that demonstrably lifts the text off the busy\n  backdrop is a legitimate pass — do not insist on a flat color block.\n- When the crop cannot settle it — JPEG/scaling artifacts, the text captured off-element or too small to\n  resolve, or a backdrop whose rendered state is ambiguous — return PARTIAL rather than guessing.\n- This rubric owns ONLY luminance/contrast readability of *text*. Do not judge font *size* (1.4.4/reflow),\n  non-text contrast (1.4.11), alt text, or focus indicators — other rubrics own those.\n\n**Output:** STRICT JSON `{verdict, confidence, summary, reasoning, evidenceRefs}`. verdict ∈\n{REPRODUCED (barrier — text not reliably readable over its backdrop), NOT REPRODUCED (no barrier — text\nclearly readable across its whole run), PARTIAL (cannot decide from the handed crops), N/A (abstain — NOT\n\"out of scope\", that is the oracle's job)}.","promptHash":"sha256:9ae4018ff070d917dc2002eb4139f3236fb5dab880bb2fa068a73e12516c2ae6"},"skill":"color-and-visual-text","element":{"xpath":"/html/body/p[1]","text":"Some invisible text in English","hasText":true,"inactiveText":false,"sectionHeading":null,"focusable":false,"isInteractive":false,"isFormField":false,"isImage":false,"ariaAttrs":[],"roleAttr":"","sampledRole":"","axRole":"","axName":"","tag":"p","type":"","href":null,"jsHref":null,"htmlSnippet":"<p style=\"position:absolute; top: -999em\">Some invisible text in English</p>","enclosingHtml":"<body>\n\t<p style=\"position:absolute; top: -999em\">Some invisible text in English</p>\n\n</body>","ariaLevel":null,"box":{"x":8,"y":-15968,"width":193,"height":18},"inModal":false,"focusRisk":false,"removedFromA11yTree":false,"hiddenMechanism":null,"renderedVisible":false,"svgLiveText":false,"svgNamedDescendant":false,"ariaHiddenWithName":false,"complexImageHint":false,"tabindexEffective":null,"hasGlyphText":false,"splitFieldGroup":false,"roleOverridesNative":false,"placeholder":null,"liveRegion":false,"isMedia":false,"mediaInfo":null,"autoMotion":false,"underOverlay":false,"hasHoverContent":false,"backgroundImageMeaningful":false,"backgroundImageUrl":null,"isCaptcha":false,"iframeTabExcluded":false,"focusableInAriaHidden":false,"prohibitedAriaAttr":false,"cdpRole":"paragraph","inTree":true,"ignoredByModal":false,"applicableScs":["1.4.3"]}}],"pOpts":{"toolsEnabled":false,"transcriptByXpath":null,"visionByXpath":{"/html/body/p[1]":{"viewport":"","viewport-320":""}},"checkerHintsByXpath":{},"file":"act:dbd2374952b96375369afe2a012bfbadd182bf6b","runId":"fp-dbd2374952b96375369afe2a012bfbadd182bf6b","pageDigest":"sha256:url:0616f272414f95fad63a6d5814a8e8ea716f06d886924639049ecfb2fc4b5e1d","llmConcurrency":40},"built":{"ok":true,"results":{"shadowObservations":[],"obligationLedger":[{"obligationId":"/html/body/p[1]::1.4.3::text-contrast","xpath":"/html/body/p[1]","sc":"1.4.3","claimFamily":"text-contrast","disposition":"PARTIAL","cleared":false,"autoPartial":true,"shadow":false},{"obligationId":"/page-level::title::2.4.2::page-title","xpath":"/page-level::title","sc":"2.4.2","claimFamily":"page-title","disposition":"PARTIAL","cleared":false,"autoPartial":true,"shadow":false},{"obligationId":"/page-level::info-relationships::1.3.1::info-relationships","xpath":"/page-level::info-relationships","sc":"1.3.1","claimFamily":"info-relationships","disposition":"PARTIAL","cleared":false,"autoPartial":true,"shadow":false},{"obligationId":"/page-level::section-headings::2.4.10::section-headings","xpath":"/page-level::section-headings","sc":"2.4.10","claimFamily":"section-headings","disposition":"PARTIAL","cleared":false,"autoPartial":true,"shadow":false},{"obligationId":"/page-level::focus-order::2.4.3::focus-order-meaning","xpath":"/page-level::focus-order","sc":"2.4.3","claimFamily":"focus-order-meaning","disposition":"PARTIAL","cleared":false,"autoPartial":true,"shadow":false}]}}}